<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William II, Elector of Hesse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a>–<a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William II, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, ruled the Electorate of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> from 1821. His autocratic rule and quarrels with his family provoked unrest, and after the revolution of 1830 he accepted a constitution and left government to his son Frederick William as co-regent. He spent his last years away from Kassel and died in 1847.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ii-elector-of-hesse.jpg" alt="William II, Elector of Hesse" width="201" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Conrad Bodenstein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_II,_elector_of_hesse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born on 28 July 1777, the eldest surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-elector-of-hesse">William I, Elector of Hesse</a>, and Wilhelmina Caroline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. In 1813 he took part with the Hessian troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-sixth-coalition">War of the Sixth Coalition</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. On 13 February 1797, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, he married Princess Augusta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the fourth daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-ii-of-prussia">Frederick William II of Prussia</a>. They had six children, three of whom died in infancy; among the others were Friedrich, the future elector, and Marie Fredericka, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-ii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen">Bernhard II, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He succeeded his father in 1821 as Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, a title that had lost its meaning with the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1806. On his accession he halted the building of the Chattenburg palace and turned instead to enlarging the Residenzpalais in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>.</p><p>His private life was entangled. With his mistress Emilie Ortlöpp, daughter of Johann Christian Ortlöpp, he had eight children; she was created Countess of Reichenbach-Lessonitz in 1821, and the children bore the title of count or countess of Reichenbach-Lessonitz. Several months after Augusta died on 19 February 1841, William married Emilie morganatically and their children were legitimated. She died less than two years later, in 1843.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Months after Emilie's death William made a third morganatic marriage, to Caroline, Baroness of Berlepsch, whom he created Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1846; the marriage was childless, and she later married Karl Adolf Graf von Hohenthal. William died on 20 November 1847 and was succeeded by his eldest surviving son, Frederick William, the last Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, so that William was the penultimate holder of the title. Through the Reichenbach-Lessonitz line his descendants include Princess Tatiana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William II, Elector of Hesse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hanau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.13,8.92&amp;year=1822" aria-label="Hanau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4754 420 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William II, Elector of Hesse"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5204 690h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5204 690h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.13,8.92&amp;year=1822">What was here in 1822? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
